ory of\""]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"theory\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Listening to enslaved and imprisoned poets changes how the lyric, its political freedoms, and its ideals of human being are understood.","confidence":0.9},"academicOrientation":{"score":78,"confidence":0.93},"profileConfidence":0.94,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"30269778f0567920aac255acf381b1e0212a9669f3af5ca4cf8cb278026bf722","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"Poetry and Bondage is a groundbreaking and comprehensive study of the history of poetic constraint. For millennia, poets have compared verse to bondage – chains, fetters, cells, or slavery. Tracing this metaphor from Ovid through the present, Andrea Brady reveals the contributions to poetics of people who are actually in bondage. How, the book asks, does our understanding of the lyric – and the political freedoms and forms of human being it is supposed to epitomise – change, if we listen to the voices of enslaved and imprisoned poets? Bringing canonical and contemporary poets into dialogue, from Thomas Wyatt to Rob Halpern, Emily Dickinson to M. NourbeSe Philip, and Phillis Wheatley to Lisa Robertson, the book also examines poetry that emerged from the plantation and the prison. This book is a major intervention in lyric studies and literary criticism, interrogating the whiteness of those disciplines and exploring the possibilities for committed poetry today.","pageCount":437,"publicationYear":2021,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Literary Criticism","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-poetry-and-bondage-a-history-and-theory-of-lyric-constraint-andrea-brady"}],"displaySummary":"For millennia, poets have compared verse to bondage – chains, fetters, cells, or slavery. Tracing this metaphor from Ovid through the present, Andrea Brady reveals the contributions to poetics of people who are actually in bondage. How, the book asks, does our understanding of the lyric – and the political freedoms and forms of human being it is supposed to epitomise – change, if we listen to the voices of enslaved and imprisoned poets? Bringing canonical and contemporary poets into dialogue, from Thomas Wyatt to Rob Halpern, Emily Dickinson to M. NourbeSe Philip, and Phillis Wheatley to Lisa Robertson, the book also examines poetry that emerged from the plantation and the prison. This book is a major intervention in lyric studies and literary criticism, interrogating the whiteness of those disciplines and exploring the possibilities for committed poetry today.","primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","primaryTopic":"Prisons & Incarceration","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Arts & Criticism","score":5,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Literary Criticism","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-poetry-and-bondage-a-history-and-theory-of-lyric-constraint-andrea-brady","id":"subject-evidence-book-poetry-and-bondage-a-history-and-theory-of-lyric-constraint-andrea-brady-1"},{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":2,"confidence":"low","note":"American history keyword; history keyword or history prize; arts/criticism keyword; crime/justice keyword","id":"subject-evidence-book-poetry-and-bondage-a-history-and-theory-of-lyric-constraint-andrea-brady-3"},{"source":"award_category","rawLabel":"Truman Capote Award for Literary Criticism","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":2,"confidence":"low","sourceId":"source-award-record-truman-capot